<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Saguntum</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 219 BCE (approx.) · capital Saguntum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Iberian town of Arse, Rome's ally, whose siege and destruction by Hannibal in 219 BCE started the Second Punic War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a>, called Arse by its inhabitants, grew in the 5th century BCE on a long rocky hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palancia">Palancia</a> river, near the coast north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, within the Edetanian world. Greek writers wrongly believed it had been founded by settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zakynthos</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>It was a prosperous Iberian town trading by sea with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> and minting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins with the legend Arse. In the 220s BCE it made an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, though it lay south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a>, the limit agreed between Rome and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-the-fair">Hasdrubal</a> in 226 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 219 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a> for eight months and took it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome's</a> declaration of war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> followed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-family">Scipios</a> recaptured the town in 212 BCE and restored its people. Saguntum flourished as a Roman municipium, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and walls still survive.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 500 BCE – 219 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:49.75%;width:5.59%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 219 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saguntum</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Iberian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Iberian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Arse, Sagunto</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-360&amp;focus=saguntum" aria-label="Saguntum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4673 757 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Saguntum lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4993 1013L4975 1081M4993 1013L4985 1015"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4975 1081h0M4985 1015h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 1013h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 1013h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-360&amp;focus=saguntum">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

